{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Which ONE of the following is a SECONDARY alcohol?\n\nA. Ethanol\nB. Butan-1-ol\nC. Butan-2-ol\nD. 2-methylbutan-1-ol"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner needs to classify the alcohol by examining the carbon bonded directly to the –OH group. Ask one focused diagnostic question without identifying an option.",
      "content": "Look at the carbon atom bonded directly to the –OH group. In a secondary alcohol, how many other carbon atoms are bonded to that carbon?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think it is one, so butan-1-ol (B) is secondary because the OH is on carbon 1."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has confused the position number of the carbon with its alcohol classification. Correct the rule, then apply it to the options and give the memo answer with a concise exam tip.",
      "content": "The position number tells you where the –OH group is, but not whether the alcohol is secondary. A secondary alcohol has its –OH-bearing carbon bonded to **two other carbon atoms**.\n\nIn butan-2-ol, the carbon bearing –OH is connected to a carbon on each side, so the correct answer is **C. Butan-2-ol**. In butan-1-ol, that carbon is at the end of the chain and is bonded to only one other carbon.\n\n**Final Answer: C. Butan-2-ol**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\mathrm{R{-}CH(OH){-}R'}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\mathrm{R}$: a carbon-containing group on one side of the carbon bearing –OH.\n  * $\\mathrm{R'}$: another carbon-containing group on the other side.\n* **Why we use it:** In this structure, the carbon bonded to –OH is attached to two other carbons, which makes the alcohol secondary. Check that carbon—not the total number of carbons in the molecule.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Don’t decide from the number in the name alone. Identify the carbon attached to –OH and count how many carbon atoms are directly bonded to it."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "C",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nanswer = sp.Symbol(\"C\")\nprint(answer)",
    "techniques": [
      "given_formula_substitute_answer",
      "concept_map"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Which ONE of the following will RAPIDLY decolourise bromine water?\n\nA. $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CHCH}_2$\nB. $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{CH}_3$\nC. $\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOCH}_3$\nD. $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}_2\\text{COOH}$"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner is stuck, so start with the key test idea rather than selecting an option. Ask one small question that focuses attention on the structural feature responsible for rapid bromine-water decolourisation.",
      "content": "Let’s take it one step at a time. Bromine water is used to test for unsaturation in organic molecules. Which type of carbon–carbon bond do you think it reacts with rapidly: a single bond or a double bond?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Maybe the acid group in D? I’m not sure."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has suggested the carboxylic acid group, so gently distinguish functional groups from the C=C bond that undergoes addition. Work the example by identifying option A as propene, show the addition reaction, and compare the remaining options. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"1 / 1\") -> 1]",
      "content": "The acid group in D is a sensible feature to notice, but it is not what makes bromine water decolourise rapidly. The key is a carbon–carbon double bond, which reacts with bromine by addition.\n\nIn option A, the structure is propene, $\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}=\\text{CH}_2$. Bromine adds across that double bond:\n$$\\text{CH}_3\\text{CH}=\\text{CH}_2 + \\text{Br}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{CH}_3\\text{CHBrCH}_2\\text{Br}$$\n\nThe orange-brown bromine colour disappears as bromine is used up. B is an alkane with only single bonds, while C and D do not have a carbon–carbon double bond like the one in A.\n\n**Final answer: A**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\text{C} = \\text{C} + \\text{Br}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{BrC} - \\text{CBr}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\text{C} = \\text{C}$: a carbon–carbon double bond in an unsaturated molecule.\n  * $\\text{Br}_2$: a bromine molecule, the coloured substance in bromine water.\n* **Why we use it:** Bromine adds across the double bond, changing it into a single bond and using up the coloured bromine. That is why bromine water is rapidly decolourised by the alkene in A.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Look for a carbon–carbon double bond when asked which substance rapidly decolourises bromine water. Do not confuse an ester or carboxylic acid group with the alkene test."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "faded_example",
    "final_answer": "A",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nanswer = sp.Symbol('A')\nprint(answer)",
    "techniques": [
      "predict_observe_explain",
      "faded_example",
      "functional_group_identification"
    ]
  }
}

