  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "A FUNCTIONAL ISOMER of ethyl propanoate is …\n\nA. $\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_9\\text{CHO}$.\nB. $\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{11}\\text{OH}$.\nC. $\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_9\\text{COOH}$.\nD. $\\text{CH}_3(\\text{CH}_2)_3\\text{CHO}$."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner may be anxious, so reduce the task to one manageable first step and avoid revealing the option. A functional-isomer question depends on both molecular formula and functional group; first diagnose whether the learner recognises the ester group from the compound name.",
      "content": "Take this one step at a time—you don’t need to solve the whole question at once. The ending **-oate** tells us the functional group in ethyl propanoate. What type of compound does that identify?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It’s an ester. I think B because it has five carbons, like ethyl propanoate?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly recognises the ester but uses carbon count alone to choose B. Address that specific misconception by comparing complete molecular formulae and functional groups. Ethyl propanoate has formula C5H10O2; option C has five carbons, ten hydrogens and two oxygens, and is a carboxylic acid. Option B is C5H12O, so it does not have the same molecular formula. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"4 + 1\") -> 5]",
      "content": "You’ve identified the ester correctly. The important exam check is that a functional isomer must have the **same complete molecular formula**, not just the same number of carbon atoms.\n\nEthyl propanoate has the formula $\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{10}\\text{O}_2$. Option B has formula $\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}$, so it does not match. Option C, $\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_9\\text{COOH}$, totals $\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{10}\\text{O}_2$ and is a carboxylic acid rather than an ester.\n\n**Final answer: C. $\\text{C}_4\\text{H}_9\\text{COOH}$**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** A functional isomer has the same molecular formula but a different functional group. For saturated, open-chain esters and carboxylic acids, the general formula is $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n}\\text{O}_2$.\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $n$: the number of carbon atoms in the molecule.\n  * C, H and O: the atoms carbon, hydrogen and oxygen; the subscripts show how many of each atom are present.\n* **Why we use it:** First find ethyl propanoate’s molecular formula, $\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{10}\\text{O}_2$. Then compare every atom in the options and check that the functional group is different. Option C matches the formula but has the carboxylic acid group, $-\\text{COOH}$, instead of the ester group.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** For the marks, show the formula match and identify the different functional group before selecting the option. Don’t choose an option based only on its carbon count; a different number of hydrogen or oxygen atoms means it is not a functional isomer."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "exam_technique",
    "final_answer": "C",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\n# Compare atom counts in ethyl propanoate and option C\nester = sp.Matrix([5, 10, 2])\noption_c = sp.Matrix([4 + 1, 9 + 1, 2])\nprint(\"C\" if ester == option_c else \"No match\")",
    "techniques": [
      "formula_matching",
      "functional_group_identification",
      "exam_technique"
    ]
  }
}

